speechtext:
No, that is not correct. There was an amount cancelled in the advances to the builders. They got into difficulties because of the rapid increase in the cost of materials, labour, and so forth, and because of what they considered to be the very stringent requirements of the owners. The contracts were drawn with certain clauses that they were to do such and such according to the requirements of the owners. The builders claimed that the owners were going much further than owners normally go in asserting rights under clauses of that kind. The situation was that some of those builders were going broke and would not have been able to finish the ships. Then the guarantee of the government would have become exigible for the very substantial portions already expended, and there would have been hulks not fitted for anything but this Chinese trade, which might have had little more than scrap value.
[Mr. St. Laurent.}
I do not remember the exact details, but the matter was gone into very carefully. Certain increases were agreed to by the owners, and there was a certain remission by the government of advances made on the cost of building the ships. A comptroller was put in one of the yards, and the department made advances to carry the job to completion. A mortgage was taken on the yard, and I know that in making the settlement there was/ a remission of a part of what had been thus expended, which was considered not to be substantial in view of what would have been the responsibility under the export insurance policy if the ships had not been completed. My information from the Department of Finance is that their information from the banks is that there has been no default on the terms under which the money was advanced by the banks.
